Suspicious Software / AV Detection
Untrusted source + AV block = quarantine the file.
Tell user to download software from the official source.
❌ Never add a suspicious file to AV exclusions just to make it run.
Key idea: Don't bypass security controls; contain → investigate → remediate.

Phishing — Suspicious Email Attachments
Check the sender domain: A legitimate company email should normally come from its official domain. Lookalike/unrelated domains are suspicious.
Be cautious with financial emails: Payment/invoice messages targeting finance staff are common phishing targets.
Password-protected archives (.rar, .zip): Attackers may use them to hide malicious files from email security scanners.
Brand impersonation: An email claiming to be from a trusted company does not make it legitimate.
Action: Block the suspicious email and investigate it.

Social Engineering — Suspicious Password Reset
Unexpected password reset + unusual contact: Possible social engineering.
Login location isn't enough: A familiar location can still be a compromised account.
User cannot confirm the request: Treat it as potentially unauthorized.
Action: Disable the account and verify the request.

Anomalous Login — Suspicious Activity
Unusual login location: A location different from the user's normal location can indicate account compromise.
Suspicious URLs before login: Misspelled/lookalike domains and unrelated websites are strong phishing indicators.
Don't dismiss anomalies based on proximity: A nearby location can still be malicious.
Action: Disable the account and investigate further.
